          "court": "Supreme Court of Hawai'i",
          "year": 2014,
          "date_decided": "2014-02-14",
          "holding": "The court held that DHS as permanent custodian has discretion in the first instance to select a child's permanent placement, that the party challenging the recommendation bears a preponderance burden, and that neither HRS chapter 587A nor Title IV-E imposes a mandatory relative-placement preference for permanent placement.",
          "relevance": "It is the Hawai'i decision that construes the federal IV-E placement provisions that federal reviews measure states against.",
          "quote": "the party challenging DHS's permanent placement recommendation bears the burden of proving by a preponderance of the evidence that the permanent placement is not in the child's best interests",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2653451/in-the-interest-of-as/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2653451
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Haaland v. Brackeen",
          "citation": "599 U.S. 255 (2023)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 2023,
          "date_decided": "2023-06-15",
          "holding": "The Court rejected the Article I, anticommandeering and recordkeeping challenges to the Indian Child Welfare Act and held the petitioners lacked standing to press the equal protection and nondelegation challenges to its placement preferences, leaving ICWA in force.",
          "relevance": "ICWA compliance and its recordkeeping requirement remain a measurable federal obligation for state agencies.",
          "quote": "Courts must adhere to the placement preferences absent \"good cause\" to depart from them.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9406660/haaland-v-brackeen/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 9406660
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Kaho'ohanohano ex rel. Morales-Kaho'ohanohano v. Department of Human Services",
          "citation": "117 Haw. 262, 178 P.3d 538 (2008)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Hawai'i",
          "year": 2008,
          "date_decided": "2008-03-20",
          "holding": "The court affirmed a negligence judgment against DHS, holding that once DHS receives a report of suspected child abuse, HRS chapter 587 and its implementing rules create a special relationship and an affirmative legal duty to investigate reasonably and to protect the reported child from further harm.",
          "relevance": "Performance failures in investigation quality that federal reviews measure are, in Hawai'i, also independently actionable in tort.",
          "quote": "Accordingly, we conclude that DHS had a duty to protect Minor under the circumstances of this case.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/6615449/kahoohanohano-ex-rel-morales-kahoohanohano-v-department-of-human/",
